[Variation "Why 3.Bb5 is indirect pressure"]

{ Volume 1: The Spanish from First Principles. Why it matters: Bb5 pins the defender of e5 only in a strategic sense: Bxc6 can remove it, but Black may recapture and still protect the pawn tactically. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Black develops, questions the bishop, and preserves the e5 center without making unnecessary concessions. White plan: White increases pressure with Re1 and c3-d4, turning the bishop's presence into central leverage. Decision: Does Bxc6 actually win e5 after every recapture and tactic, or is maintaining tension more valuable? Danger: Counting attackers and defenders without calculating the zwischenzug or changed pawn structure. Memory rule: Bb5 pressures the defender so White can build the center; it does not simply win e5. } *

