[Variation "The value of the e5 strongpoint"]

{ Volume 1: The Spanish from First Principles. Why it matters: Black's e5 pawn claims central space and restricts White, but it needs support; White's whole setup asks how to increase pressure without releasing tension. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 { d6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. Black plan: Reinforce e5, castle, and prepare counterplay that makes White spend time away from d4. White plan: White plays c3 and d4, sometimes h3 first, to challenge the pawn under ideal conditions. Decision: Which piece is doing a real job in the defense of e5, and can White distract or exchange it? Danger: Defending e5 with passive pieces until Black has no counterplay. Memory rule: A strongpoint is useful when it supports activity, not when every piece is chained to it. } *

