[Variation "Useful tension versus premature exchange"]

{ Volume 1: The Spanish from First Principles. Why it matters: The Spanish position stores energy in unresolved choices: Bxc6, d4, ...b5, and ...d5. Timing determines who benefits from the release. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. Black plan: Black keeps the center stable and creates queenside space while preparing a freeing break. White plan: White keeps the bishop, supports d4, and waits until an exchange improves piece placement or pawn structure. Decision: If the tension is released now, which side gains a tempo, file, or favorable recapture? Danger: Exchanging merely to simplify the position without identifying the improved piece or structure. Memory rule: Keep tension when the opponent must keep solving it. } *

