[Variation "Closed Spanish center"]

{ Volume 2: Spanish Pawn Structures. Why it matters: The intact e4-e5 tension keeps both sides maneuvering; White prepares d4 while Black looks for ...d5 or queenside activity. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 { h3: White prevents a pin and supports the coming central plan at the cost of one tempo. Black plan: Black improves pieces behind the chain, restrains d4, and prepares a central break that does not abandon e5. White plan: White reroutes the b1 knight through d2-f1-g3 and builds d4 under maximum control. Decision: Which side benefits if d4 or ...d5 happens now, and whose pieces are better placed for the opening center? Danger: Launching a wing operation without checking whether the center can open against the king. Memory rule: In a closed Spanish center, maneuver to improve the coming pawn break. } *

