[Variation "Open e-file transformation"]

{ Volume 2: Spanish Pawn Structures. Why it matters: Black releases the e5 strongpoint and accepts open lines in return for active central pieces and immediate play. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 Be6 { Be6: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Use central activity and development to justify the pawn capture; keep the king safe as the e-file opens. White plan: White uses the pin, c3, and Nbd2 to pressure the centralized knight and the d5 base. Decision: Can the e4 knight retreat safely after White's forcing sequence, and what activity does Black gain? Danger: Taking e4 because it is available without calculating d4 and the attack on the knight. Memory rule: An open center rewards activity; a pawn grab must develop the rest of the army. } *

