{ Volume 2: Spanish Pawn Structures. Why it matters: Black accepts doubled c-pawns and the bishop pair; White obtains a healthier queenside majority and a long-term endgame target. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 { dxc6: Black releases tension by capturing on c6; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 5. O-O f6 6. d4 exd4 { exd4: Black releases tension by capturing on d4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 7. Qxd4 Qxd4 8. Nxd4 { Nxd4: White releases tension by capturing on d4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. Black plan: Use the bishops and central majority before simplification fixes the pawn weaknesses. White plan: White exchanges pieces, restrains Black's active breaks, and creates a kingside pawn majority in the ending. Decision: Does the proposed exchange increase the bishop pair's activity or merely approach White's favorable pawn ending? Danger: Trading every piece because the position looks equal while the pawn ending is structurally inferior. Memory rule: In the Exchange Spanish, Black needs active bishops before the endgame becomes only pawns. } *

