{ Volume 3: Map of the Ruy Lopez Families. Why it matters: Black maintains e5 and retreats the c6 knight to reorganize, accepting space pressure in exchange for a resilient center. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 Nb8 { Nb8: Black reroutes a knight to b8; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Reroute pieces, prepare ...d5, and create queenside or kingside activity according to White's setup. White plan: White completes c3-d4 and improves knights before opening the position. Decision: Which black piece has the least useful future, and what route gives it a central job? Danger: Calling a retreat passive without seeing the square and break it prepares. Memory rule: Spanish retreats are investments: name the destination and the break they support. } *

