{ Volume 3: Map of the Ruy Lopez Families. Why it matters: Black takes e4 and builds a broad active center; White receives targets and tactical pressure against the centralized knight. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 Be6 { Be6: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 9. c3 Bc5 { Bc5: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Use development and central space, support the e4 knight, and calculate every d5 or f7 tactic. White plan: White attacks the center with c3, Nbd2, and tactical pins before Black consolidates. Decision: Which central pawn or knight is loose after White's next forcing move? Danger: Believing central activity excuses an undefended piece or delayed king safety. Memory rule: In the Open Spanish, activity is the compensation and must remain concrete. } *

