{ Volume 4: The c3-d3 Spanish System. Why it matters: d3 keeps e4 secure and delays central clarification, giving White time for Nbd2-f1-g3 and flexible kingside play. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. d3 { d3: White changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. Black plan: Black gains room for ...Na5, ...c5, or ...d5 and should use the extra time to challenge White's compact center. White plan: White improves pieces, plays h3, Nbd2-f1-g3, and chooses d4 only when it wins a favorable transformation. Decision: What did d3 concede in central space, and which maneuver compensates for it? Danger: Playing the universal setup without noticing Black has achieved ...d5 in one move. Memory rule: A quiet center buys maneuvering time but gives the opponent central freedom. } *

