{ Volume 4: The c3-d3 Spanish System. Why it matters: The b1 knight often reaches g3 through d2 and f1, supporting f5, h5, and central squares without blocking the c-pawn. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. d3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 { c5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 11. Nbd2 { Nbd2: White reroutes a knight to d2; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Black uses the maneuvering time for ...c5, ...Re8, and pressure that limits the knight's destination. White plan: White accepts several tempi because the final g3 knight coordinates an attack and frees other pieces. Decision: Is g3 the knight's real destination, and what useful action can Black take during the journey? Danger: Following the route when f1 or g3 is tactically unavailable or strategically irrelevant. Memory rule: A reroute is justified by the job at the destination, not by tradition. } *

