{ Volume 4: The c3-d3 Spanish System. Why it matters: White waits until pieces support d4 and Black's pieces have declared themselves, then challenges e5 with maximum coordination. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. d3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 { c5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 11. Nbd2 Re8 12. Nf1 Bf8 { Bf8: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 13. d4 { d4: White challenges e5 and opens lines after the preparatory pieces are ready. Black plan: Black decides whether to maintain tension, exchange, or counter with ...d5 based on piece placement. White plan: White opens lines for bishops and rooks while preventing Black from gaining a free central break. Decision: After d4, which recapture preserves the better center and which file opens for each rook? Danger: Playing d4 by schedule when the pieces are not ready for the resulting open center. Memory rule: Break only when your pieces improve after the pawn exchanges. } *

