{ Volume 5: Closed Spanish Foundations. Why it matters: h3 prevents ...Bg4 from increasing pressure on d4 and gives the king luft, but it also spends a tempo and slightly weakens g3. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 { h3: White prevents a pin and supports the coming central plan at the cost of one tempo. Black plan: Black uses the tempo to reorganize and prepares ...Na5, ...Nb8, or ...Bb7 according to the chosen system. White plan: White makes d4 easier and keeps the f3 knight free from a pin during central expansion. Decision: Is ...Bg4 a real problem in this move order, and what does White gain by stopping it now? Danger: Calling h3 useful without accounting for the development tempo it gives Black. Memory rule: A prophylactic move earns its place by protecting a future plan. } *

