{ Volume 5: Closed Spanish Foundations. Why it matters: c3 prepares d4, gives the bishop c2 after ...Na5, and controls b4 and d4; it also takes the c3 square from the knight. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 { Bc2: White changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Black uses ...Na5 to gain the bishop pair or force Bc2, then expands with ...c5 against d4. White plan: White preserves the bishop and builds the d4 break with coordinated pieces. Decision: Which future square does c3 reserve for the bishop, and how will the b1 knight develop instead? Danger: Playing Nc3 and c3 plans simultaneously without noticing the square conflict. Memory rule: In the Spanish, pawn moves often reserve routes for pieces two moves later. } *

