{ Volume 5: Closed Spanish Foundations. Why it matters: Both sides delay capture because the recapture changes piece activity. White wants d4 with space; Black wants a version where exchanges free the cramped position. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 Nb8 10. d4 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Reinforce the center and prepare ...Bb7, ...Re8, or ...Bf8 before releasing tension. White plan: White increases central control and may advance d5 to gain space if Black cannot break. Decision: Which side's worst piece improves after the central exchange? Danger: Resolving tension to reduce calculation while giving the opponent a free developing recapture. Memory rule: Release the center when the recapture helps you more than the opponent. } *

