{ Volume 6: Closed Spanish Maneuvering. Why it matters: ...Nb8 clears c6, reroutes to d7, supports e5, and prepares coordinated ...Bf8 and ...g6; the retreat solves several future problems. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 Nb8 10. d4 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 11. Nbd2 Bb7 12. Bc2 Re8 { Re8: Black places a rook behind a file or pawn break expected to become important. Black plan: Complete the reroute, defend e5 economically, and seek ...d5 when the pieces support it. White plan: White uses the time for d4, Nbd2-f1-g3, and queenside space, trying to make the reroute too slow. Decision: What new square and pawn break become possible because c6 was vacated? Danger: Imitating ...Nb8 without knowing whether the Breyer structure and timing are present. Memory rule: A backward move is strong when it clears one square and improves two future moves. } *

