{ Volume 6: Closed Spanish Maneuvering. Why it matters: The knight reaches g3 where it supports f5, h5, e4, and f1 while leaving the c-pawn's work intact. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 Nb8 10. d4 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 11. Nbd2 Bb7 12. Bc2 Re8 { Re8: Black places a rook behind a file or pawn break expected to become important. } 13. Nf1 Bf8 14. Ng3 { Ng3: White reroutes a knight to g3; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Black restrains f5 and uses ...g6, ...c5, or ...d5 before White completes a kingside buildup. White plan: White accumulates pieces toward the king while preserving central tension. Decision: What attacking or central break does Ng3 make possible, and what can Black challenge now? Danger: Completing a long maneuver while the center changes and the destination loses relevance. Memory rule: Recheck the board at every station of a maneuver; the destination is not guaranteed. } *

