{ Volume 6: Closed Spanish Maneuvering. Why it matters: The bishop retreats to c2 to preserve itself and aim at h7, but it can become buried behind e4 unless the center opens. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 { c5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 11. d4 Qc7 12. Nbd2 { Nbd2: White reroutes a knight to d2; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Black blocks or contests the diagonal and uses queenside space while monitoring kingside sacrifices. White plan: White opens the b1-h7 diagonal with d4 and sometimes e5, then coordinates queen and bishop against h7. Decision: Is the bishop currently active, and which pawn break turns its hidden diagonal into a threat? Danger: Valuing the bishop pair abstractly while the c2 bishop has no line or target. Memory rule: Preserve the Spanish bishop only if you can eventually open its diagonal. } *

