[Variation "The central pawn sacrifice"]

{ Volume 7: The Marshall Attack. Why it matters: ...d5 gives a pawn to open diagonals, accelerate development, and pull White's pieces away from king defense. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d5 { d5: Black challenges the center or offers dynamic activity, depending on the system. } 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 { Nxe5: Black releases tension by capturing on e5; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 11. Rxe5 c6 { c6: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. Black plan: Use the lead in activity, bishop pair, and queen entry squares before White consolidates the extra pawn. White plan: White returns material or exchanges attackers, giving priority to king safety over keeping every pawn. Decision: After accepting the pawn, which black piece enters with tempo and which defensive piece can White trade? Danger: Black drifting after the sacrifice or White greedily defending material while lines open around the king. Memory rule: The Marshall pawn buys time and lines; spend both immediately. } *

