{ Volume 7: The Marshall Attack. Why it matters: Qh4 creates direct threats on h2 and coordinates with bishops; White's pieces must defend squares, not merely count material. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d5 { d5: Black challenges the center or offers dynamic activity, depending on the system. } 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 { Nxe5: Black releases tension by capturing on e5; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 11. Rxe5 c6 12. d4 Bd6 { Bd6: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 13. Re1 Qh4 { Qh4: Black coordinates several plans, but the queen's safety and the opponent's forcing reply must be checked. Black plan: Increase forcing pressure with bishops and rooks while preventing simplifying exchanges. White plan: White plays g3 or h3 accurately, offers material back, and tries to exchange the dark-squared bishop or queen. Decision: What is the immediate threat after ...Qh4, and which defense also improves coordination? Danger: Answering the visible mating threat while allowing a second tactical entry on h2 or f2. Memory rule: In the Marshall, list all threats before choosing a defensive move. } *

