{ Volume 7: The Marshall Attack. Why it matters: White's extra pawn has little value if the king remains boxed in; returning it can trade queens or attacking pieces and reach a playable ending. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d5 { d5: Black challenges the center or offers dynamic activity, depending on the system. } 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 { Nxe5: Black releases tension by capturing on e5; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 11. Rxe5 c6 12. d4 Bd6 { Bd6: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 13. Re1 Qh4 14. g3 Qh3 { Qh3: Black coordinates several plans, but the queen's safety and the opponent's forcing reply must be checked. Black plan: Black avoids premature exchanges and keeps threats linked, but must recognize when the attack has ended. White plan: White creates luft, covers h2, and offers material to eliminate the most dangerous attacker. Decision: Which material return removes the largest share of Black's attacking force? Danger: Trying to refute the attack tactically when a calm material return solves the king-safety problem. Memory rule: Against compensation, return material when it buys coordination and king safety. } *

