{ Volume 8: Anti-Marshall Systems. Why it matters: a4 attacks the queenside chain and changes Black's ...d5 sacrifice because b5 can become loose. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 O-O 8. a4 Bb7 { Bb7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 9. d3 Re8 { Re8: Black places a rook behind a file or pawn break expected to become important. Black plan: Black maintains development, protects the queenside, and switches toward central or kingside piece play. White plan: White fixes a queenside target, keeps the center compact, and later chooses c3-d4. Decision: Does a4 win a target now, or does it merely force Black into a different stable structure? Danger: Assuming the Marshall is avoided and relaxing about Black's central activity. Memory rule: Avoiding one system means accepting another; know what a4 weakens and attacks. } *

