{ Volume 8: Anti-Marshall Systems. Why it matters: The d3 center denies immediate central liquidation and leads to maneuvering where queenside weaknesses and piece routes matter. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 O-O 8. a4 Bb7 { Bb7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 9. d3 Re8 10. Nbd2 Bf8 { Bf8: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Black uses ...Bf8, ...g6, or ...d5 preparation and avoids defending b5 passively. White plan: White pressures b5, reroutes knights, and chooses d4 when the pieces are ideally placed. Decision: Which side can improve a piece without fixing a new weakness? Danger: Obsessing over the b5 pawn while the opponent takes over the center. Memory rule: In anti-Marshall positions, the game returns to Spanish maneuvering; improve the worst piece. } *

