{ Volume 9: The Berlin Defense. Why it matters: Queens come off, but the position remains rich: White has better pawns and space; Black has bishops, king activity, and pressure on e5. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 { Nxe4: Black releases tension by capturing on e4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 5. d4 Nd6 6. Bxc6 dxc6 { dxc6: Black releases tension by capturing on c6; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 7. dxe5 Nf5 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 { Kxd8: Black releases tension by capturing on d8; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. Black plan: Black activates the king and bishops, connects rooks, and undermines e5 before White consolidates. White plan: White restricts the bishops, protects e5 efficiently, and uses the kingside pawn majority. Decision: Whose slow asset becomes active first: Black's bishops or White's pawn majority? Danger: Playing automatically because the queens are gone and allowing the opponent's strategic asset to mature. Memory rule: The Berlin trades tactical danger for a race between activity and structure. } *

