{ Volume 10: The Open Spanish. Why it matters: Black can take e4 because ...d5 supports the knight and opens active lines; the apparent pawn grab is a prepared central system. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 Be6 { Be6: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Keep the knight coordinated, develop with tempo, and use the broad center before White attacks its base. White plan: White challenges the center immediately and uses pins against c6 and tactical pressure on d5. Decision: After White's next forcing move, where can the e4 knight go and which pawn holds the center together? Danger: Taking e4 in a move order where ...d5 cannot support the knight. Memory rule: The Open Spanish pawn capture is sound because the center arrives behind it. } *

