{ Volume 10: The Open Spanish. Why it matters: Black's center may later dissolve, so development and piece activity must create threats while it exists. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Nxe4 6. d4 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 Be6 { Be6: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 9. c3 Bc5 10. Nbd2 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. Black plan: Castle, centralize rooks, and use bishops on active diagonals before accepting simplification. White plan: White seeks exchanges that leave Black with weak central pawns and no initiative. Decision: Does the proposed exchange preserve Black's activity or leave only structural targets? Danger: Trading active pieces because it simplifies calculation while the center becomes weak. Memory rule: Dynamic compensation expires; convert it into development, tactics, or a sound structure. } *

