{ Volume 13: Chigorin and Zaitsev Systems. Why it matters: Black gains the bishop retreat and attacks White's center with ...c5, accepting that the a5 knight may need a later route. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 { c5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 11. d4 Qc7 { Qc7: Black coordinates several plans, but the queen's safety and the opponent's forcing reply must be checked. Black plan: Use queenside space and c-file pressure, then reroute the a5 knight before it becomes irrelevant. White plan: White builds d4, may play d5, and uses the a5 knight's distance from the kingside. Decision: After ...c5, where is the a5 knight's next useful square and what happens if the center closes? Danger: Completing ...Na5-c5 by habit while the knight has no return route. Memory rule: A gain of bishop tempo is temporary; budget a route for the knight. } *

