{ Volume 13: Chigorin and Zaitsev Systems. Why it matters: ...Bb7 and ...Re8 intensify pressure on e4 and prepare active central play without retreating the c6 knight. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 Bb7 10. d4 Re8 { Re8: Black places a rook behind a file or pawn break expected to become important. } 11. Nbd2 Bf8 { Bf8: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Coordinate the bishop and rook against e4, then use ...Bf8 and ...h6 or ...g6 to stabilize the king. White plan: White uses d5, a4, or Ng5 ideas to exploit the exposed long diagonal and move order. Decision: Does pressure on e4 create a real tactical obligation, or can White advance d5 with gain of time? Danger: Adding attackers to e4 while ignoring White's central advance or repetition resources. Memory rule: Pressure matters when it changes the opponent's move; otherwise improve the worst piece. } *

