{ Volume 13: Chigorin and Zaitsev Systems. Why it matters: Chigorin creates immediate queenside and c-file play; Zaitsev keeps more central pressure and piece tension. The choice changes the long-term knight problem. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 { c5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 11. d4 Qc7 12. Nbd2 { Nbd2: White reroutes a knight to d2; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Select the system whose piece route and central break match the current move order, not merely personal taste. White plan: White adapts the center: d5 against queenside play, or tactical pressure against e4-focused pieces. Decision: Which black knight or bishop will be worst after the center closes? Danger: Mixing moves from both systems and producing a position where neither plan works fully. Memory rule: A system is a coordinated package; do not collect its moves one at a time. } *

