{ Volume 14: Schliemann, Bird, and Classical Defenses. Why it matters: ...f5 challenges e4 immediately and creates tactical imbalance, but weakens the king and e6 while development is incomplete. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 f5 4. d4 fxe4 { fxe4: Black releases tension by capturing on e4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 5. Nxe5 Nf6 6. O-O Be7 { Be7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Use the open f-file and central activity, develop quickly, and calculate checks before keeping extra material. White plan: White opens the center, targets the king, and uses rapid development against the weakened dark squares. Decision: After ...f5, does Black have a safe king and enough development for every opened line? Danger: Treating surprise value as compensation for a permanently exposed king. Memory rule: A gambit defense succeeds through activity; if development stops, the weaknesses remain. } *

