{ Volume 14: Schliemann, Bird, and Classical Defenses. Why it matters: ...Nd4 offers a structural transformation that gains central space but can leave the d4 pawn overextended and the c-pawn committed. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 Nd4 4. Nxd4 exd4 { exd4: Black releases tension by capturing on d4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 5. O-O c6 6. Be2 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Use the space to develop with tempo, support or exchange the d4 pawn, and avoid a static target. White plan: White exchanges, attacks d4, and uses the open e-file plus faster castling. Decision: Is the d4 pawn a space-gaining wedge or a target that blocks Black's own pieces? Danger: Defending the advanced pawn with more pawn moves while falling behind in development. Memory rule: An advanced central pawn must restrict pieces or open lines; otherwise it is a target. } *

