{ Volume 14: Schliemann, Bird, and Classical Defenses. Why it matters: ...Bc5 develops actively and allows a more immediate central confrontation than the Morphy Defense. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. d4 exd4 6. e5 Ne4 { Ne4: Black reroutes a knight to e4; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 7. cxd4 Bb4+ { Bb4+: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Use piece activity and tactical checks to meet d4; do not let the bishop become a target without compensation. White plan: White builds c3-d4 with tempo and uses e5 to drive the f6 knight. Decision: After White's central advance, which active check or capture preserves Black's initiative? Danger: Leaving the bishop on c5 while White gains d4 and e5 with tempo. Memory rule: Active development must anticipate the pawn moves that attack it. } *

