{ Volume 15: The Exchange Variation. Why it matters: ...f6 supports e5 and prepares king activity, but it blocks the g8 knight's best square and slightly loosens the king. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 { dxc6: Black releases tension by capturing on c6; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 5. O-O f6 6. d4 exd4 { exd4: Black releases tension by capturing on d4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 7. Qxd4 Qxd4 8. Nxd4 Bd7 { Bd7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Develop through e7 or h6, use the bishop pair, and avoid letting f6 become a permanent hook. White plan: White opens the center while Black's kingside development is awkward and seeks exchanges. Decision: Which route develops the g8 knight and king most efficiently after ...f6? Danger: Protecting e5 so heavily that the kingside pieces never become active. Memory rule: A pawn that supports the center must not imprison the pieces behind it. } *

