{ Volume 15: The Exchange Variation. Why it matters: If all pieces disappear, White can often create a passed pawn with the kingside majority while Black's doubled c-pawns fail to make one. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 { dxc6: Black releases tension by capturing on c6; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 5. O-O f6 6. d4 exd4 { exd4: Black releases tension by capturing on d4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 7. Qxd4 Qxd4 8. Nxd4 Bd7 { Bd7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 9. Be3 O-O-O { O-O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. Black plan: Keep active pieces, create counterplay, and exchange into pawn endings only after calculating the majority race. White plan: White welcomes piece trades that preserve a healthy kingside four-against-three majority. Decision: After all exchanges, can each wing create a passed pawn and whose king reaches it first? Danger: Calling a queenless or rookless ending equal without calculating the pawn majorities. Memory rule: Before simplifying, play the pawn ending in your head. } *

