{ Volume 16: Ruy Lopez Tactical Grammar. Why it matters: Pressure on e5 often depends on whether the c6 knight is pinned and whether Black has a tactical reply against e4. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. d4 Bg4 { Bg4: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Unpin when necessary, defend e5 with active pieces, and use counter-pressure instead of piling up passive defenders. White plan: White combines Bxc6, dxe5, and tactical pressure on pinned pieces to win central time. Decision: If the c6 knight moves or disappears, what becomes loose on e5, d4, or b5? Danger: Using simple attacker-defender counting while a pin changes which piece can legally recapture. Memory rule: In Spanish tactics, ask whether the c6 knight is a defender in fact or only on paper. } *

