{ Volume 16: Ruy Lopez Tactical Grammar. Why it matters: ...a6, ...b5, ...Na5, and ...c5 can gain bishop tempi, but the famous trap pattern works only if the bishop truly lacks a safe square. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 Na5 { Na5: Black reroutes a knight to a5; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 9. Bc2 c5 10. d4 Qc7 { Qc7: Black coordinates several plans, but the queen's safety and the opponent's forcing reply must be checked. Black plan: Gain space while tracking the bishop's retreat route and the central cost of each pawn move. White plan: White preserves the bishop via c2 and uses d4 to attack the pawn chain before it advances again. Decision: If the queenside pawns advance one more time, where exactly can the bishop retreat? Danger: Playing the trap pattern by rote while White opens the center and escapes with tempo. Memory rule: A trapping net is real only when every flight square is controlled. } *

