{ Volume 17: Spanish Endgames and Transformations. Why it matters: Black's bishops need open diagonals; if pawns become fixed on one color, one bishop may become a large pawn while White attacks targets. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 { dxc6: Black releases tension by capturing on c6; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 5. O-O f6 6. d4 exd4 { exd4: Black releases tension by capturing on d4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 7. Qxd4 Qxd4 8. Nxd4 Bd7 { Bd7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 9. Be3 O-O-O { O-O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. Black plan: Create pawn breaks, keep both bishops active, and avoid fixing every pawn on the bishop's own color. White plan: White blockades, exchanges the active bishop, and attacks the doubled pawns with knights and rooks. Decision: Which pawn break opens a diagonal for the restricted bishop? Danger: Keeping the bishop pair as a label while the structure denies both bishops useful lines. Memory rule: The bishop pair is an asset only when the position can open. } *

