{ Volume 17: Spanish Endgames and Transformations. Why it matters: Black's doubled c-pawns often cannot create a clean passed pawn, while White's kingside majority can; piece activity must alter that arithmetic. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 { dxc6: Black releases tension by capturing on c6; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 5. O-O f6 6. d4 exd4 { exd4: Black releases tension by capturing on d4; recalculate the pawn skeleton, open lines, and the opponent's most forcing recapture. } 7. Qxd4 Qxd4 8. Nxd4 Bd7 { Bd7: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. } 9. Be3 O-O-O 10. Nc3 { Nc3: White reroutes a knight to c3; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. Black plan: Use the c-pawns for square control or exchanges, activate the king, and create counterplay before the distant majority advances. White plan: White fixes the c-pawns, centralizes the king, and prepares a kingside passer. Decision: Can Black trade one doubled pawn for a healthy enemy pawn and repair the majority? Danger: Treating doubled pawns as immobile weaknesses rather than potential levers and exchange tools. Memory rule: Damaged pawns can still buy squares and exchanges; spend them actively. } *

