{ Volume 18: A Lifetime Ruy Lopez System. Why it matters: White's durable plan links pressure on e5, c3-d4, bishop preservation, and knight rerouting; each move prepares the central release. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 Nb8 10. d4 Nbd7 { Nbd7: Black reroutes a knight to d7; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 11. Nbd2 Bb7 12. Bc2 Re8 { Re8: Black places a rook behind a file or pawn break expected to become important. } 13. Nf1 Bf8 { Bf8: Black changes a bishop's diagonal; identify the central pawn move that will make that diagonal useful. Black plan: Black organizes a freeing break and challenges White's buildup before every piece reaches its ideal square. White plan: White identifies the least active piece, improves it, and plays d4 only when the transformation helps the whole army. Decision: Which preparatory move is still essential, and which can be omitted because Black changed the setup? Danger: Reciting the full maneuvering script after the position has already demanded concrete action. Memory rule: The Spanish plan is modular: keep the purpose, adjust the move order. } *

