{ Volume 18: A Lifetime Ruy Lopez System. Why it matters: Black's major systems answer the same pressure differently: maintain e5, sacrifice with ...d5, enter a queenless Berlin, or activate through an Open Spanish center. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d5 { d5: Black challenges the center or offers dynamic activity, depending on the system. Black plan: Choose a system whose strategic debt you accept, then play its coordinated package rather than mixing move fragments. White plan: White identifies the debt: weak squares, sacrificed pawn, uncastled king, or delayed development, and builds pressure against it. Decision: What did Black gain, what permanent concession was made, and how must the gain be used? Danger: Selecting a defense by reputation while disliking the middlegames it intentionally creates. Memory rule: Every Spanish defense is a bargain; know both the asset and the debt. } *

