{ Volume 18: A Lifetime Ruy Lopez System. Why it matters: Because the Ruy Lopez branches deeply, efficient study begins at the first position where your plan became unclear, not at move one after every loss. } 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Nc6: Black reroutes a knight to c6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 { Nf6: Black reroutes a knight to f6; judge the move by the job at its destination and the time conceded on the way. } 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 { b5: Black gains bishop tempo and queenside space while creating a possible pawn target. } 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O { O-O: Black secures the king and connects a rook; central and wing operations can now be judged more concretely. } 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 { c5: Black changes the pawn skeleton; reassess breaks, weak squares, open lines, and king exposure. } 11. d4 Qc7 { Qc7: Black coordinates several plans, but the queen's safety and the opponent's forcing reply must be checked. Black plan: Record the structure, candidate moves, and strongest opponent reply; add one corrected line and one verbal rule to the repertoire. White plan: Use both colors in training so opponent resources become familiar plans rather than surprises. Decision: At what move did you stop understanding the position, and what cue should trigger the correct plan next time? Danger: Replacing understanding with ever-longer files or abandoning the opening after one tactical loss. Memory rule: Repair the first unexplained decision and rehearse it from both sides. } *

